From: Noa Himesaka <himesaka@noa.codes>
Subject: [PATCH] Compose Korean syllables in the preedit

Qt Virtual Keyboard's HangulInputMethod never uses a preedit: for every
jamo it reads the character before the cursor from the surrounding text
and commits the recomposed syllable with a replacement range. We can only
forward that as delete_surrounding_text + commit_string, and the
surrounding text we have lags behind the application by a compositor
round trip. With a stale or missing previous character the jamo is
committed on its own (ㅎㅓ instead of 허), and Firefox applies the delete
against stale text as well (Mozilla bug 2080478), doubling the input.

Add HangulPreeditInputMethod, which does what ibus-hangul and
fcitx5-hangul do: libhangul's 2-set automaton composes the syllable in the
preedit, and only finished syllables are committed. Committed text is
never rewritten, so no delete_surrounding_text is sent at all.

Qt Virtual Keyboard asks the input method to commit whenever the
surrounding text changes outside of an input method event. Here the
application's report of what we sent always arrives later, and may be one
or more of our actions behind, so the input method remembers the states it
put the application in and recognises reports of them (and briefly trusts
the result of keys it passed on, like Return or Backspace) instead of
breaking every syllable in two. Qt leaves the preedit out of the
surrounding text; Firefox includes it, with the cursor after it, so a
report like "험" while "허" is being committed and "마" shown is ours too.
Any other change, e.g. tapping elsewhere, ends the composition without
committing it again, since applications commit their preedit themselves
then (Firefox, and Qt on a mouse click; a Qt Quick field tapped by touch
keeps it uncommitted, so that syllable is lost).

It lives in org.kde.plasma.keyboard.lib so the KCM can load the ko_KR
layout too.
